you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  a warning that arctic sea ice could be  melting up to twice as fast  as previously thought  racing to win and to save the planet  how sailors in ocean race europe are  helping to collect  vital data about the health of our seas  and the herd of elephants 300 miles from  home  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions now some parts of the arctic  could have no ice by 2040 according to  new research from scientists at  university college london  they found that parts of sea ice in the  region are thinning up to twice as fast  as previously thought and that could  have a big impact on the rate of global  warming  the likelihood of extreme weather events  and flooding  in coastal communities well julienne  strouver is professor of polar  observation and modeling at ucl and  joins me now  welcome to you tell us more then about  your findings just how  serious is the situation in the arctic  well i think what was so novel about the  work that we did is that we're really  trying to get a better handle on how  much the ice is thinning because we have  over four decades of observations about  how much of the arctic ocean is covered  by sea ice  but it's been a lot harder to measure  how thick that ice is  and while we have satellites in space at  the moment that can  give us some ideas of sea ice thickness  a key unknown has always been how much  snow is on top of the ice and so  assumptions have been made that the snow  depth is on the order of what was  measured back in the  you know 1970s 1980s at north pole  drifting stations  but we know that that's probably no  longer accurate in today's arctic  because for one the sea ice forms so  much later than it used to  and so it takes you don't have as much  time over which snow can accumulate on  the sea ice  and so on a different project we had  produced a  more temporally varying snow depth  product based on combining  atmospheric re-analyses with a new snow  model to  sort of simulate how much snow is on the  ice and when you put  that data product into the retrievals  for measuring sea ice thickness  we found that when we do that yeah the  sea ice is thinning much faster than we  expected  and it's not too surprising but it was  nice to be able to quantify that this  is indeed the case and what are the  ramifications for that  what does it mean for climate change  well i mean obviously we've always been  concerned about well how quickly is the  arctic ocean going to transform  to a seasonally ice free arctic ocean  and you know we look at our climate  model outputs and they all suggest  sometime by the middle of the century  but you know if the ice is thinning  faster than we think then it could even  happen a bit sooner than what the models  are forecasting and so i think  it definitely has implications for yeah  the timing of when we might start seeing  arctic ice free conditions  and of course the arctic does not act  alone in our climate system what happens  in the arctic  doesn't really stay in the arctic it can  have implications for the planet as a  whole and exactly  how that plays out of course is still  quite uncertain  julianne struver thanks very much indeed  thank you  let's take a look now at some of the  day's other climate news and new  analysis shows that electric  and hybrid cars have seen another boost  in sales this month  one in five of all cars sold in may in  the uk  were hybrid and sales of electric cars  have grown from 2224  to 11 769 in just  one year now that's an increase of more  than 500 percent  in the northeast electric car sales have  increased from four percent of the  market  to 11 the un  is launching its decade of restoration  campaign which hopes to use the next 10  years to halt the degradation of  ecosystems  and restore them to achieve global goals  the body has hosted a major event with  world leaders including the german  chancellor angela merkel  and pakistani prime minister imran khan  u.n secretary general antonio guterres  had this to say  the task is monumental we need to  replant and protect our forests  we need to clean up our rivers and seas  and we need to green our cities  accomplishing these things will not only  safeguard the planet's resources  it will create millions of new jobs by  2030 generate returns of over  7 trillion us dollars every year and  help eliminate poverty and hunger  russian president vladimir putin has  said that it's a myth  that russia isn't concerned about  climate change  and that melting permafrost and coastal  erosion were risks  speaking at an economic conference in  saint petersburg he also defended the  use of natural gas  claiming that it's clean and should be  used in the transition to cleaner energy  a tsunami that hit scotland over 8 000  years ago would have devastated coastal  towns and cities  if it happened today according to  researchers the sterega tsunami  was caused by glacial underwater  landslides along norway's continental  shelf  in the norwegian sea triggering a major  displacement of water  modelling shows the wave could have  traveled up to 30 kilometers inland  along the scottish coast a hoard of  elephants that have trekked more than  300 miles away from their home in  southwest china could have done so  because of a decline in food in their  natural forest habitat  the 15 wild elephants have now arrived  at the densely populated city of kunming  the capital of yunnan province along  their journey they've caused around a  million dollars of damage  now 20 people from thorac and basildon  in essex have formed a climate jury  they're aiming to influence development  in the area whilst  ensuring environmental and community  needs are met  the jury have put forward 32 proposals  aiming to create a prosperous and  low-carbon future  their suggestions include investment in  cleaner and affordable public transport  as well as schemes allowing employees  days off to restore  nature well to talk more about the  proposals builder and climate juror  danny pierce joins me now  hello to you first of all why did you  decide you wanted to get involved in a  climate jury  i'll be honest with you um i was just  sent a letter randomly um  saying i could be involved in it and um  i've put my opinions forward so  it was currently like they've been  locked down so i thought why not let's  just have a go  see what it's like and i'll be honest  with you i was quite pessimistic at  first and  i didn't think it'd be something i'd be  too interested in but  as the weeks were going on i found  myself getting deeper  and they got more interested into it and  um  i was actually like looking forward to  the meetings and when it ended i  actually felt like something had been  achieved  at the end of it well that's fantastic  so you feel it's been a useful process  tell us a little bit about some of the  ideas that you all came up with  any favorites um  i'll be honest with y'all um i think so  cycle routes  uh uh was like a big one that kept  coming up and um  where they're planning to change over  like new production of uh  make all vehicles electric like cars by  um  2030 there was a there was a big issue  about  like the charging of them vehicles in  certain areas  so that that was an interesting one and  uh i think that's one that  definitely needs to be sort of addressed  going forward  and do you think you'll take anything  away from it personally about  how you live your life and how you do  your work you're a builder aren't you  any any lessons from what you've learned  during the process  um with myself i'm a little bit  i'm a bit restricted but i know that  there ain't there ain't many options at  the moment for myself or any  affordability for um  an electric van but it'd be it would be  useful and i'd definitely consider it if  they brought in  um almost like a scrappage scheme  um for like to trade in older vans  to replace them with like more more um  greener vans  i'd be definitely saying that'll benefit  myself as a builder and i'm definitely  saying i'll be interested in  interesting stuff well danny pierce we  really appreciate your time thanks very  much indeed  no problem thank you for having me the  second leg of the ocean race  europe kicks off this weekend and  several of the teams taking part will be  doing  more than just racing some of the boats  are carrying scientific equipment on  board  to measure the impact of climate change  on the seas and the amount of  microplastics in the water  while the three-week race started in  l'oreal in france on the 29th of may  before heading to  qashqai in portugal where the teams are  now now they'll set off from there this  weekend  racing to alicante in spain  the race finishes with genova in italy  on the 19th of june  and the data collected at every stage  will be  provided to scientific organizations  studying and mapping the impact of the  climate crisis on our oceans  well joining me now from qashqai is  simon fisher navigator for the 11th hour  racing team  uh welcome to you so tell us first of  all about this scientific research  element of the race tell us more about  what you're doing yeah thanks very much  for  being on um yeah it's actually really  exciting um  we're we're doing this three race and uh  we're obviously you know competing to  win but  also a real pleasure and um  an honor to also contribute to the  science uh to science community while  we're racing around i've  been racing in these boats or in this in  this  offshore racing for a long time now so  now i'm sort of getting to the veteran  stage of my uh sailing series nice to be  able to give something back  as someone who's got a bit of a science  background it's really nice that we can  we can contribute to  the science community well yes i think i  i've read that you're a  chemistry graduate aren't you so that's  obviously been part of the appeal  but what exactly is the research looking  at i mean you're looking at the quality  of the water aren't you but  are you also studying marine mammals as  well  yeah so um we uh yeah we have an ocean  pack  i can send the camera a little and you  can see this uh big box of scientific  equipment and  that's constantly as we're racing that's  constantly pumping water through the  boat and uh  measuring uh dissolved carbon dioxide uh  sea temperature uh salinity so uh  sea water salinity which are all really  metrics for for  gauging uh you know climate change the  uh  the the ocean actually absorbs like 90  percent of the excess heat that we break  as uh  the human race and uh and and the ocean  is also  carbon sink but um obviously the more  carbon the ocean absorbs it  the ocean absorbs the the bigger effect  it has on on the ecosystems and marine  life so  uh it's also hugely variable so i think  the more we can do understand that and  provide data it is very very valuable  and uh yeah we also uh you know but also  the eyes and the ears for the scientists  out here  specifically with marine mammals we all  really like well i'd  love to see the sea life and it's a real  pleasure when we see dolphins whales  birds  and that stuff when we when we're racing  and i'm sure but uh  actually by writing that down noting  noting observations wearing them  the frequency and things like that we  can actually provide a really valuable  resource to  the science community well simon fisher  it sounds  extraordinary good luck with the science  and good luck with the race as well  thanks very much indeed  thank you thanks for having me on well  that's it for today's show  but you can get your climate fix over  the weekend with our podcast  and on this week's episode we're talking  climate influencers with love island  star er booker  and katya jones from strictly that's on  climatecast and it's available wherever  you normally get your podcast from  and on our weekly digital show we go  behind the scenes of our correspondent  siobhan robbins is filming  with the divers collecting discarded  fishing gear and turning it into ppe  in thailand that'll be available on sky  news social channels our app and website  from saturday morning thanks for  watching see you next week  you

